# Single source of truth for the vendored BusyBox pin (M21.5 — the `scp-runner-dep` image).
#
# Shell-sourceable (`. tools/busybox/pin.env`) AND parsed verbatim by:
#   - apps/runner-dep/Dockerfile   (the FINAL `FROM` — must match BUSYBOX_PINNED_IMAGE)
# `packages/plugins/managed-dep/src/runner-image.test.ts` asserts the Dockerfile agrees with this
# file, and `runner-image.integration.test.ts` asserts the BUILT IMAGE agrees with both — see
# tools/busybox/README.md ("Updating the pin") before changing anything here.
#
# WHY THIS IMAGE AT ALL. `scp-runner-dep` is the ISOLATED EDITOR the `scp-managed-dep` orchestrator
# launches per bump. Four clauses of the charter's 2026-08-13 `scp-managed-dep` amendment ("never
# runs a package manager", "never resolves or regenerates a lockfile", "never builds, compiles, or
# tests", "the runner contains no package manager") are properties of what the image CONTAINS, not
# of any restraint the orchestrator can exercise. BusyBox is one static binary providing `sh`, `awk`
# and `sed` — it carries no compiler, no language runtime, and no package manager OF ITS OWN (no
# apk, no dnf), which is a stronger posture than either sibling runner's base.
#
# WHY THE INDEX DIGEST, NOT THE amd64 PLATFORM DIGEST, FOR THE `FROM` — identical reasoning to
# tools/trivy/pin.env and tools/openscap/pin.env, and for the identical reason: this image is
# EXECUTED as an ephemeral runner (unlike cosign/skopeo, which are binaries copied OUT of an image
# that is never executed), so the multi-arch INDEX digest keeps the pull content-addressed and
# reproducible while resolving to the BUILD HOST's architecture. The linux/amd64 platform digest —
# the production runner target arch — is recorded below for provenance and asserted by the drift
# test.
#
# WHY A DIGEST AND NOT THE TAG. `busybox:1.36.1-musl` is a MUTABLE tag: Docker Hub republishes
# maintenance rebuilds under it, so the same Dockerfile would produce different bytes over time in
# an image that is handed somebody else's source file and whose whole security argument is what it
# does NOT contain. The digest is what makes "this image has no toolchain in it" a statement about
# a fixed artifact rather than about whatever the tag resolved to on the build host that day.

# The BusyBox release this pin corresponds to (the upstream tag the digests below were resolved
# from). Recorded for provenance; the build consumes the digest, never this.
BUSYBOX_PINNED_VERSION=1.36.1-musl

# The upstream repository (docker.io/library/busybox). Recorded so the drift test can assert the
# Dockerfile FROM names exactly this repo at exactly the pinned digest.
BUSYBOX_UPSTREAM_REPO=busybox

# The multi-arch INDEX (manifest-list) digest — consumed by apps/runner-dep/Dockerfile's `FROM`.
# Resolves per build-host arch; content-addressed and reproducible.
BUSYBOX_PINNED_IMAGE=busybox@sha256:3c6ae8008e2c2eedd141725c30b20d9c36b026eb796688f88205845ef17aa213

# The linux/amd64 PLATFORM manifest digest for this index (the production runner target arch) —
# recorded for provenance and asserted by the drift test; nothing consumes it at build time.
BUSYBOX_PINNED_AMD64_DIGEST=sha256:cbf412bcf1379481c80f65208703910fe543b3a948ae74a32a10ca3789dc13ab
